<section class="container" style="padding-block:4rem 2rem;max-width:760px;">
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">“Eva Mann” carries a deliberate semantic tension in the name itself: “Eva” — understood internationally — stands prototypically for the female sex. “Mann” (man) is understood in both German and English and has a prototypically male connotation. The brand draws its inherent memorability from precisely this tension between the two parts of the name — a name that sticks precisely because it unites two opposing associations.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Lünstroth has developed and managed the brand for the fashion label over many years: direction, push-in materials, trade fair communication, trade advertisements, lookbooks and the website — the full range of what a fashion brand continuously needs towards retail, press and end customers.</p>
</section>

